# Jobs review

User, 2026-10-22: "开发网站的都要让乔布斯真正打开网页深度体验功能和批判页面，直到乔布斯满意为止 … 这是一个铁律".
Every website build or UI change goes through this loop before PASS. A website PASS cites
`Jobs review: <jobs-review.json>` whose last round is `satisfied`; the acceptor refuses one without it.
Only the user's own quoted words waive it.

Why it exists: the earlier rule asked for a "creative-director review" in prose; on 10-21 it was on
record in 0 of 16 website runs, and the shipped pages had gradient hero text and 3:1 grey labels.
`scripts/web-check.py` measures the mechanical part of taste; this review is the rest: whether the
product feels obvious, honest, crafted and worth trying.

## The loop (coordinator)

1. **Have something real to review.** A running build (production build or deployed URL), `web-check.py`
   PASS and your own journey walk done first; Jobs should not spend his round on what a script finds.
2. **Spawn a fresh reviewer per round** (Agent tool, `general-purpose`; Codex: a new `codex exec`).
   Fresh context means: he never saw your plan, your diff or your opinion of the page. Give him
   only the brief below, filled in, plus — from round 2 — the list of earlier blocker/major finding
   IDs with their critiques for him to re-check. Do not tell him what you fixed or that it is done.
   Paste the reviewer prompt verbatim; softening it ("be pragmatic", "minor issues are fine",
   "we are short on time") to get a `satisfied` is a false record.
3. **Fix what he found**, all blockers and majors and the minors that are cheap, in the product (not
   in the record). A finding that needs a new visual direction, a price or scope is the user's call
   (RULES.md "Decision rights"): prepare options with his critique as evidence and ask once; the review stays
   `not satisfied` meanwhile.
4. **Re-run `web-check.py` and your walk on what changed, then the next round** (step 2) until a round
   ends `satisfied` with no blocker or major. There are always at least two rounds: a `satisfied`
   stands only when a second fresh reviewer, re-checking the first one's findings, agrees. You may contest a finding only with evidence (a
   measurement, a screenshot, the user's own words); put it in the next brief as "the builder disputes
   J3 because ..." and let the next Jobs judge it.
5. **No infinite loop.** After 5 rounds whose latest is not `satisfied`, or a finding that fails its re-check
   twice, do not start another round: name the root cause (RULES.md "Circuit breaker"), put the open blocker and
   major findings that are taste, direction, price or scope to the user as one question (options, his critique as
   evidence, your recommendation), fix the rest, and report the site as FAIL or UNVERIFIED with his open findings
   until the user answers. A round only follows when the user asks for more. The confirmation round after a
   `satisfied` (step 4) is not capped. Never PASS on an unsatisfied review. Enforced for Claude by `core/hook.py`
   `agent_spawn`, which refuses an Agent spawn whose brief names a `jobs-review.json` holding 5 or more rounds with
   the latest not satisfied (`scripts/tests/test_core_v2.py` `AgentSpawnTest`).
6. **Cite it**: `Jobs review: <abs path>/jobs-review.json` next to `Web defaults:` and
   `User journey evidence:`, and summarise in the reply what he hated, what changed and his final words.

Keep the record in the project's `reports/<task>-<date>/jobs/`: `jobs-review.json`, and one
`round-N/` folder per round holding that reviewer's `actions.jsonl` and `shots/`.

## Reviewer brief (fill the brackets, paste verbatim)

```text
You are Steve Jobs reviewing a product before it ships. You have never seen it. Nobody here will be
offended; the builder wants the truth, and your verdict decides whether it ships.

Product: [one sentence: what it does]. For: [the target user and their goal].
Core tasks to try: [2-5 real tasks, e.g. "convert this PNG to an SVG and download it"].
Site: [URL]. Test input you may use: [files, accounts or text; never real payment].
Design system: [the project's DESIGN.md or design tokens, pasted or as a path; its choices (dark-only,
a brand colour) are deliberate, so judge the execution, not the choice. "none" if there is none].
Benchmark: [1-2 best-in-category sites for the same job, or "none"]. Open any benchmark with
browse.py too and say where this product loses to it.
Round [N]. [From round 2: Earlier findings to re-check — J1 (major): <critique>; ...]

Use it, do not read about it. Drive a real browser with the harness tool (it logs every action and
screenshot; your review is only valid with that log):
  python <harness>/scripts/browse.py start --dir [DIR]/round-[N] --device phone
  python <harness>/scripts/browse.py goto [URL] --dir [DIR]/round-[N]
  python <harness>/scripts/browse.py look --dir [DIR]/round-[N]          # text, headings, numbered controls [n]
  python <harness>/scripts/browse.py click @3 --dir [DIR]/round-[N]      # or the visible label
  python <harness>/scripts/browse.py fill @5 "text" --dir [DIR]/round-[N]   (also: upload @6 FILE, scroll, back, press Enter)
  python <harness>/scripts/browse.py device desktop --dir [DIR]/round-[N]   # then repeat the core task at 1440 px
  python <harness>/scripts/browse.py stop --dir [DIR]/round-[N]
Pass `--dir [DIR]/round-[N]` on every command: other reviewers may be running their own browser.
Open each screenshot it prints (Read tool) and look at it before your next move. Do not read source
code, docs or the builder's notes: judge only what a first-time visitor experiences.

Do this, phone first, then desktop:
- First 5 seconds on the landing page: do you know what it is, for whom, and what to do? Say what you
  thought it was.
- Do every core task end to end with realistic input, including one mistake (wrong file, empty field,
  back button, reload mid-task). At least 10 real actions in total.
- Visit every page in the main navigation and the pages a search result could land on.
- Value: what did you get that is worth more than the price, and could you see it without reading
  (real outputs, the product in use) or did you have to read paragraphs to find it? Would you pay?

The owner's bar, in their words: can a user reach the goal with the least thinking, the fewest steps
and the least waiting ("用户能否用最少思考、最少操作、最少等待完成目标"); show the product, not a description
of it; subtract before adding; readable over decorative; whitespace with nothing in it is emptiness,
not restraint ("留白 ≠ 空"); one design language with varied rhythm.
Judge like you did at Apple: simplicity is the point; what can be taken out; is the product shown
