Sources

The games are inspired by Wittgenstein's writing; they do not summarise it. Each passage below is quoted briefly, read in plain English, and paired with a note on where the game's analogy stops. Section numbers (§) refer to the numbered remarks; Tractatus numbers are proposition numbers.

Play the games
Philosophical Investigations

tr. G. E. M. Anscombe (short quotations for teaching)

§2

“A is building with building-stones: there are blocks, pillars, slabs and beams. B has to pass the stones, and that in the order in which A needs them.”

Wittgenstein imagines a complete language of four words. 'Slab!' does not need a definition: it works because B brings a slab.

Where the game stops: His builders are a thought experiment, not a theory of how children learn.

Read the original text

§6

“An important part of the training will consist in the teacher's pointing to the objects, directing the child's attention to them, and at the same time uttering a word; for instance, the word "slab" as he points to that shape.”

You had no word for the curved stone, so you pointed and said 'this'. Pointing plus a word is how the builders' vocabulary grows.

Where the game stops: Wittgenstein adds that pointing only works for someone already trained in the game; a gesture alone does not fix a meaning (§§28–30).

Read the original text

§7

“I shall also call the whole, consisting of language and the actions into which it is woven, the 'language-game'.”

Words and actions together make a language-game. You just played one.

Read the original text

§8

“Let us now look at an expansion of language (2). Besides the four words "block", "pillar", etc., let it contain a series of words used as the shopkeeper in (1) used the numerals … further, let there be two words, which may as well be "there" and "this" … that are used in connexion with a pointing gesture”

Add numbers and pointing words, and the same four-word game can do much more: 'Two slabs — there!' ('Connexion' is an old spelling of 'connection'.)

Read the original text

§11

“Think of the tools in a tool-box: there is a hammer, pliers, a saw, a screw-driver, a rule, a glue-pot, glue, nails and screws.—The functions of words are as diverse as the functions of these objects. (And in both cases there are similarities.)”

Tools share no single job, yet they are not a random heap: there are similarities. Words are like that too.

Read the original text

§14

“Imagine someone's saying: "All tools serve to modify something. … "—And what is modified by the rule, the glue-pot, the nails?”

You can force one definition onto every tool, but only by twisting the words. The ruler was the counterexample.

Read the original text

§§19–20

“…is the call "Slab!" in example (2) a sentence or a word?”

A one-word order is not broken English. Inside a shared activity, 'Slab!' does the same job as 'Bring me a slab'.

Read the original text

§21

“We could imagine a language in which all statements had the form and tone of rhetorical questions; or every command the form of the question "Would you like to …?"”

The grammatical form of a sentence does not fix its job. 'Would you like to…?' can offer, invite or instruct.

Read the original text

§23

“Here the term "language-game" is meant to bring into prominence the fact that the speaking of language is part of an activity, or of a form of life.”

Giving orders, asking, thanking, joking, describing: these are different games, even when they use the same words.

Read the original text

§27

“Think of exclamations alone, with their completely different functions. Water! Away! Ow! Help! Fine! No! Are you inclined still to call these words "names of objects"?”

“Slab!” shouted as a warning is not naming a stone. One-word calls do many different jobs.

Read the original text

§43

“For a large class of cases—though not for all—in which we employ the word "meaning" it can be defined thus: the meaning of a word is its use in the language.”

Note the caution: 'not for all'. Here, what 'It's a bit cold in here' meant depended on what it was being used to do.

Where the game stops: This is not a slogan that meaning is always and only use.

Read the original text

§66

“Don't say: "There must be something common, or they would not be called 'games'"—but look and see whether there is anything common to all. … To repeat: don't think, but look!”

Instead of hunting for a definition of 'game', look at the actual games and how they overlap.

Read the original text

§67

“I can think of no better expression to characterize these similarities than "family resemblances" … the strength of the thread does not reside in the fact that some one fibre runs through its whole length, but in the overlapping of many fibres.”

Your chain held together although no single feature ran through every card. That is a family resemblance.

Where the game stops: Wittgenstein does not say no word has a definition; he says we should not assume one.

Read the original text

§185

“Now we get the pupil to continue a series (say +2) beyond 1000—and he writes 1000, 1004, 1008, 1012.”

Every example the pupil had seen fitted his way of going on too. Your pupil did the same with 'an' before a vowel letter.

Where the game stops: The game's deviant ways are a short, fixed list; Wittgenstein's point is that no finite list of examples rules out every one.

Read the original text

§198

“…any interpretation still hangs in the air along with what it interprets, and cannot give it any support. Interpretations by themselves do not determine meaning.”

A loose explanation was one more sign to interpret, and the pupil read it its own way. The precise one worked only after your examples had ruled out the pupil's own reading: the explanation needed the practice behind it.

Where the game stops: The pupil's few ways of going on are a toy model. Wittgenstein's point is not that explanations fail, but that none works without a practice of using it (§§198–202).

Read the original text

§§201–202

“And hence also 'obeying a rule' is a practice.”

What settles the next step is not a formula in the pupil's head but the shared practice of English speakers — which is why sometimes the right call was 'both are in use'.

Where the game stops: This is not scepticism: Wittgenstein does not say any answer is as good as any other.

Read the original text

§217

“If I have exhausted the justifications I have reached bedrock, and my spade is turned. Then I am inclined to say: "This is simply what I do."”

Why does English choose 'a' or 'an' by sound and not by spelling? At some point the reasons stop, and there is just what English speakers do.

Read the original text

§133

“The real discovery is the one that makes me capable of stopping doing philosophy when I want to.”

The later Wittgenstein's version of the ladder: the point is not to keep climbing, but to be able to stop.

Read the original text

Part II, xi (PPF §§113–118)

“I contemplate a face, and then suddenly notice its likeness to another. I see that it has not changed; and yet I see it differently. I call this experience "noticing an aspect".”

The duck-rabbit does not change on the page; what changes is how you take it. Ambiguous English sentences flip the same way.

Where the game stops: Wittgenstein writes about seeing pictures; extending it to sentence structure is our analogy.

Read the original text

§109
