Learn English by playing Wittgenstein's language-games

Five short games for intermediate learners. You give orders, describe scenes, read a room and teach a pupil — and the English, and the philosophy, come from what you do.

Play Slab!
All five games
Notebook

No account. Your progress stays in this browser.

Slab!

The builder calls. Bring him what he asks for:

block
pillar
slab
beam
The games
Slab!
Fetch stones for a builder — then give the orders yourself.
Picture Theatre
Build the scene a sentence describes, catch false reports.
The Café
Same words, different jobs. Read the room, then respond.
Go On!
Teach a stubborn pupil a spelling rule with as few examples as you can.
Family Threads
What do all games have in common? Don't think — look.
Why Wittgenstein?

In the Philosophical Investigations, Wittgenstein asks us to look at language as part of what people do: builders calling for stones, shopkeepers counting apples, someone asking, thanking, joking. He called these activities language-games. Each game here turns one of his examples into something you play, so you learn the English the way he says words are learned: by using them.

The games are inspired by his writing, not a summary of it. After each session you get the passage, a plain-English reading, and a note on where the game's analogy stops. All sources

“The limits of my language mean the limits of my world.”
Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus 5.6, tr. C. K. Ogden
