qa/playtest-codex/scene-pupil\actions.jsonl:182:{"device": "phone", "round": "pupil-1-3", "screenshot": "phone-pupil-1-3-judged.png", "text": "Skip to content\nGo On!\n\nTeach the pupil\n\n1\nTeach the pupil\n2\nRules with no rule\n\nTHE PUPIL'S TEST\n\nstopped\nliked\nvisited\npreferred\nplayed\n\nTeach the pupil to spell the past tense.\n\nThe stress in “travel” is on the first syllable, so the pupil writes “traveled”. Your British textbook says “travelled”.\n\nGood call. Both are in use: American English writes “traveled”, British English keeps “travelled”. Two communities, two practices; neither is the pupil's mistake.\n\nEnglish practised: Past -ed spelling: drop silent e, double a final consonant after a short stressed vowel.\n\nNext\nAbout Go On!\nHow to play\nA pupil sits a five-word test, using its own idea of the rule.\nShow it example cards. After each one it re-sits the test with whatever rule fits everything it has seen.\nGet all five right with as few cards as you can — then decide what to do with a word that breaks the pattern.\n\nEnglish you practise: a/an, plurals, -ed, comparatives, -ing or to, uncountables.\n\nThe idea\n\nEvery example the pupil had seen fitted his way of going on too. Your pupil did the same with 'an' before a vowel letter.\n\nPhilosophical Investigations §185. Read the passage"}
